y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(3)=2$\n- **II.** $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(3)=4$\n- **III.** $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^3y}{dx^3}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(3)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(3)=0$" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496595 1072 #uuid "04cab1c1-b4c4-563e-a4e0-aea26627ab39" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496595 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(3)=2.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(3)=4.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $(5+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^3y}{dx^3}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(3)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(3)=-1,\\,{y}^{″}(3)=0.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496596 1080 "true" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496596 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496596 1083 #uuid "05c33bf8-24f2-5808-8fcd-56b9362db81d" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496597 1080 "true" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496597 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535329 1