}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(2)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3$" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496603 1072 #uuid "210dbb55-04e5-5206-8aa8-e837c31cd3f9" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496603 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=-4.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $(3+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=3.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496604 1080 "false" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496604 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496604 1083 #uuid "21932082-6385-5ffa-b2e9-f613c507a0ea" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496605 1080 "true" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496605 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496605 1083 #uuid "2e418261-4a43-56f5-ab4b-4fa6bf1f181b" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496612 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496606 1075 #edb/ref 17592186496617 13194139535329 175921