ect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(4)=-2$\n- **II.** $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-1y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- **III.** $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{d^2y}{dx^2}-3y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496623 1072 #uuid "9be539bf-0592-5c27-a363-62e029d0bec0" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496623 1074 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(4+x^2)\\left(\\dfrac{dy}{dx}-2y\\right)+x{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-2.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496624 1080 "false" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496624 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496624 1083 #uuid "a12833f2-4e47-5632-b3a8-fc9f1744a73b" 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496606 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496629 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496625 1068 #edb/ref 17592186496640 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535329 17592186045443 true] [17592186496625 1071 "Which of the following s